    45	## Key Technical Decisions
    46	
    47	- **KTD1 — Surface-weighted velocity-averaging dissipation (the mechanism).** In `g2p_water`, set `v = mix(v_own, v_grid, c)` where `v_grid = Σ w·grid_v` (today's value, the local average), `v_own = vel[p] + gravity·dt` (the particle's retained momentum), and `c` is the **surface-weighted smoothing strength**: `c → 1` (full average, calm) in the bulk, `c → c_surface` (small, momentum-preserving, splashy) near the free surface. This is the grid form of XPBD's XSPH-with-surface-falloff (`src/solvers/xpbd/water.wgsl:226-267`). Prior art: ASFLIP (Fei et al., *Revisiting Integration in the MPM*, SIGGRAPH 2021 — APIC + adaptive FLIP, blend rises on separation); the XSPH≡PIC-fraction unification; Yao & Zhao 2026 (compression–vorticity blend) as a documented signal refinement.
    48	- **KTD2 — Signal = separation vs compression (ASFLIP), NOT density/support alone. (Load-bearing — this is what prevents main's drip-stir.)** main keyed preserve on `support_ratio` (≈ local density), so a *slow, sparse* drip read as "free," kept its momentum, punched into the cup pool, and the velocity projection rang it up into a spontaneous stir/blow-up. **Local density has the same flaw** (a drip is sparse) — density-only keying would reproduce main's failure. So the **primary** discriminator is the **separation/compression sign**: preserve (low `c`) only for *separating or neutral* motion (a crown flying out, a coherent free droplet); **dissipate (high `c`) for compressive motion** — a particle moving *into* a denser/slower neighborhood (a drip merging into the pool). The compression indicator is the **local velocity divergence ∇·v = trace of the velocity gradient `B` already gathered in `g2p_water`** for the affine `C` (`transfers.wgsl` — free, no new gather): merge ⇒ ∇·v < 0 ⇒ dissipate ⇒ no stir; crown ⇒ ∇·v > 0 ⇒ preserve ⇒ splash; ballistic free fall ⇒ ∇·v ≈ 0 ⇒ preserve (harmless, no pool to stir). Local grid density (`grid_vel[node].w`) is a **secondary** gate only (compression matters near dense neighbors); the `near_air` mask is an optional sharpener. Prior art: ASFLIP separation condition (Fei 2021 — α rises on separation, falls in compression); Yao & Zhao 2026 compression suppression `χ = max(0,−∇·v)`.
    49	- **KTD3 — Generalize `pic_blend`, do not replace it.** The affine-`C` blend (`pic_blend = 0.05`, `mod.rs:151`) stays; this adds the **velocity-side** surface weighting that two-field currently lacks. In the bulk the new term reduces to today's behavior, so the settled-pool quiet and the **crater-hold decouple proof** (`tests/twofield_full.rs` — production holds, blend-0 slumps, cohesion-off slumps) are preserved by construction.
    50	- **KTD4 — Plumbing: no layout break, no new buffer.** Promote the prototype consts into `Params` using the **documented-unused** `box_min.w` / `box_max.w` / `gravity.w` lanes (keeps the 256-byte `Params` assert intact — `mod.rs:358`); add `Config` fields; default-off so the off-path is byte-identical and all existing gates stay green. The averaging uses the existing per-particle `vel[p]` (`common.wgsl:68`) — **no new storage buffer**. `g2p_water` has no `workgroupBarrier`, so the per-particle branch is **Tint-uniformity-safe**.
    51	- **KTD5 — Velocity clamp on the splash path, with FP-safety (supporting).** The surface crown's peak velocity must survive the `max_speed` cap (currently 12 in the web scenes). Relax it on the splash path at the clamp sites (`transfers.wgsl` p2g + g2p, `coupling.wgsl:292` drag_fold, `plasticity.wgsl:420` solid_update), **keeping the fixed-point overflow backstop** (headroom ∝ `8192/max_speed`, `common.wgsl:117`) and gating the relaxation with cap-hit-rate + FP-saturation probes (extend `tests/twofield_water.rs`). Not a blanket removal.
    52	- **KTD6 — Scope: transfer lever only; stay two-field.** Two-field is kept for its speed/UX (user decision). The incompressibility-pressure lever (over-pack R9, over-buoyant coffee R13, the impact-**absorption** half of "mush") and jet geometry (R2) are a **separate pressure follow-up** — the in-repo `dbg.w` two-sided density-target is the planned path there (already built; compliance diagonal already rejected; a global Poisson risks the checkerboard/operator-inconsistency that killed main's solver against thin perf headroom). Not in this plan.
